<Guide slug="turn-clipart-into-a-coloring-activity">
  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <img
      src="/studio/botanical-sprig.svg"
      alt="An editable curved stem with leaves and berries."
      width="800"
      height="600"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Start with the Botanical sprig studio starter. Its stem, leaves, and berries can be edited
      separately.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h2>Give the activity a clear purpose</h2>
  <p>
    A coloring page needs space to work. This project uses one botanical sprig as a focal point
    instead of filling the sheet with small decorations. A child can choose a palette, add a
    caption, or compare the curved stem with the pointed leaves. This is a creative activity
    suggestion, not a tested lesson plan or a claim about learning outcomes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The main decision is how much detail to keep. The curved stem is distinctive, but fine surface
    accents can compete with the broad shapes when reduced to a small print. The editor lets you
    hide those layers and compare the result without changing the original download.
  </p>
  <h2>1. Start with the real drawing</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/create/">Open the creative studio</a>, choose <strong>Botanical sprig</strong>, then
    open <strong>Layers</strong> and inspect the named pieces. Hide decorative surface details one
    at a time if you want a simpler activity. Keep the main stem and several well-spaced leaves so
    the subject remains recognizable. Save your project before trying a different direction.
  </p>
  <h2>2. Compare grayscale with outlines</h2>
  <p>
    Grayscale removes color but retains dark filled areas. Outline mode changes the treatment of
    shapes so there is more open space to color. Open <strong>Print / B&amp;W</strong> and compare{' '}
    <strong>Grayscale</strong> with <strong>Outline only</strong>. They solve different problems:
    grayscale is useful for a monochrome illustration, while a coloring activity usually benefits
    from open interiors.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not assume a single preset makes every drawing suitable. Look where the leaves meet the stem
    and where small berries overlap. If small contours become confusing, hide the relevant accent
    layer or increase the print size. Use Undo if you remove a line that helps explain the shape.
    You can return to the original colors by choosing the Botanical sprig starter again after saving
    your project.
  </p>
  <h2>3. Add a short prompt with room to answer</h2>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Text</strong>, add a caption such as “My leaf colors,” and position it away from
    the leaves. Keep a broad blank area around the sprig if the activity includes writing or
    drawing. A caption should not cover the subject or make a child color around tiny letters.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a digital worksheet, export a transparent WebP and arrange it in your worksheet application.
    Place the instructions as real text in that document where possible. A single flattened image
    containing all the instructions is harder to adapt and does not provide the same accessibility
    as selectable document text.
  </p>
  <h2>4. Check the whole printed page</h2>
  <p>
    For a direct print, open <strong>Print setup</strong>, choose US Letter or A4, and start with
    half-inch margins and <strong>Fit entire artwork</strong>. Enable the paper preview. Fit keeps
    the complete composition inside the available area; Fill page can cut off the stem or leaf.
    Export PDF and open the downloaded file before printing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The PDF uses CMYK vector shapes, but a printer's “black and white” option may interpret gray or
    colored elements differently. If ink use matters, inspect the page and print a single proof. We
    have not physically tested this activity on every printer or paper. Avoid promising a particular
    ink saving based only on the on-screen preview.
  </p>
  <h2>Make the second version meaningfully different</h2>
  <p>
    Try a large single sprig for free coloring, then a smaller version with more blank space for an
    original drawing. These are adaptations of the same artwork, not two newly authored catalog
    illustrations. Keep the project files if you want to change the layout again. Personal and
    educational adaptations are permitted; resale of the artwork or commercial use is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    For help choosing an export, read{' '}
    <a href="/guides/webp-pdf-and-editable-projects/">Which file should you download?</a>. If the
    printed lines look soft, compare your exported vector PDF with a screenshot: the screenshot has
    a fixed pixel count and can lose detail when enlarged.
  </p>
</Guide>
